<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.7.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02440</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.7.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>64b8a33d-763f-43a4-a2ec-a4d7cae7b704</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Mai and Jada are solving the equation \(2x^2−7x=15\) using the quadratic formula.</item>
</list>
<para>Answer the following questions about the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) if this equation is written in standard form, \(ax^2+bx+c=0\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(a\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of \(b\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -7</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the value of \(c\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -15</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Mai and Jada are solving the equation \(2x^2−7x=15\) using the quadratic formula but found different solutions.</item>
  
  <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>Mai's Method</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Jada's Method</emphasis></entry></row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para>
            \(x=\frac{-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(2)(-15)}}{2(2)}\) 
            \(x=\frac{-7 \pm \sqrt{49-(-120)}}{4}\) 
            \(x=\frac{-7 \pm \sqrt{169}}{4}\) 
            \(x=\frac{-7 \pm 13}{4}\) 
            \(x=\frac{-7+13}{4}=\frac{6}{4}=\frac{3}{2}\) 
            \(x=\frac{-7-13}{4}=\frac{-20}{4}=-5\)
          </para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>
            \(x=\frac{-(-7) \pm \sqrt{(-7)^2-4(2)(-15)}}{2(2)}\) 
            \(x=\frac{7 \pm \sqrt{49-(-120)}}{4}\) 
            \(x=\frac{7 \pm \sqrt{71}}{4}\)
          </para>
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
  
  <para>Who has the correct solution?</para>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Mai</item>
  <item> Jada</item>
  <item> Both of them </item>
  <item> Neither one </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Neither one
  Mai used 7 for \(b\) instead of -7. For \(b^2\) in the formula, Jada computed \(-7^2\) instead of \((-7)^2\), so she wrote -49 instead of 49.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>The equation \(h(t)=-16t^2+80t+64\) represents the height, in feet, of a potato \(t\) seconds after it was launched from a mechanical device.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that would allow us to find the time the potato hits the ground.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(0=16t^2+80t+64\) </item>
  <item> \(0=-t^2-5t+4\) </item>
  <item> \(0=-16t^2+80t+64\) </item>
  <item> \(0= t^2-5t-4\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(0=-16t^2+80t+64\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation without graphing and write it down. Enter your solution for the approximate time the potato hits the ground rounded to one decimal place.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5.7</para>
<para>\(t \approx 5.7\). Using the quadratic formula, with \(a=-16\), \(b=80\), and \(c=64\), the potato hits the ground about 5.7 seconds after being launched. (The negative solution has no meaning in this situation.)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Priya found \(x=3\) and \(x=-1\) as solutions to \(3x^2−6x−9=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>If you check Priya's answer by substitution, is the answer correct?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Yes </item>
  <item> No </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
<para>Substituting both 3 and -1 for \(x\) in the original quadratic expression and then evaluating the expression gives a value of 0.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>If you check Priya's answer by graphing, is the answer correct?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Yes </item>
  <item> No </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
<para>Graphing the equation \(y=3x^2−6x−9\) shows \(x\)-intercepts at \((3,0)\) and \((-1,0)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>If you check Priya's answer by using the quadratic formula, is the answer correct?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Yes </item>
  <item> No </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
<para>Substituting 3 for \(a\), -6 for \(b\), and -9 for \(c\) in the quadratic formula gives \(x=3\) and \(x=-1\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>A frog jumps in the air. The height, in inches, of the frog is modeled by the function \(h(t)=60t−75t^2\), where \(t\) is the time after it jumped, measured in seconds.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions to the equation \(60t−75t^2=0\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> 0 </item>
  <item> \(\frac{4}{5}\) </item>
  <item> 5 </item>
  <item> 1 </item>
  <item> \(-\frac {1}{5}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0, \(\frac{4}{5}\)</para>
<para>\(t=0\), \(t=\frac{4}{5}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What do the solutions tell us about the jumping frog? <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> that apply.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> The frog landed 1 seconds after it jumped and was on the ground again. </item>
  <item> The frog landed \(\frac{4}{5}\) seconds after it jumped and was on the ground again. </item>
  <item> The height of the frog was 5 inches before it jumped. </item>
  <item> The negative solution shows how far down the frog was before it jumped. </item>
  <item> The height of the frog was 0 inches before it jumped. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The frog landed \(\frac{4}{5}\) seconds after it jumped and was on the ground again. The height of the frog was 0 inches before it jumped.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>A tennis ball is hit straight up in the air, and its height, in feet above the ground, is modeled by the equation \(f(t)=4+12t−16t^2\), where \(t\) is measured in seconds since the ball was thrown.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions to the equation \(0=4+12t−16t^2\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(\frac{1}{4}\) </item>
  <item> 4 </item>
  <item> 16 </item>
  <item> 1 </item>
  <item> \(-\frac{1}{4}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1, \(-\frac{1}{4}\)</para>
<para>\(t=1\), \(t=-\frac{1}{4}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What do the solutions tell us about the tennis ball? <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> that apply.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> The ball hits the ground \(\frac{1}{4}\) second after it is hit. </item>
  <item> The negative solution doesn't have meaning because a negative value for time doesn't make sense. </item>
  <item> The ball hits the ground 1 second after it is hit. </item>
  <item> The negative solution is a result of the bounce of the ball. </item>
  <item> The ball hits the ground \(-\frac{1}{4}\) second after it is hit. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The negative solution doesn't have meaning because a negative value for time doesn't make sense. The ball hits the ground 1 second after it is hit.</para></section>
